3426: \section{Evidence boundary}
3427: 
3428: \subsection{Primary--supplement interface}
3429: 
3430: \paragraph{Submission-interface checklist.}
3431: For this presentation-only route, the primary reviewed artifact is
3432: \path{submission_abstract.tex} and the long record is \path{main.tex}.  The short
3433: artifact must expose the following five interface components so that reviewers
3434: can check it against the supplement without reading an abstraction proof:
3435: \begin{enumerate}
3436: \item an abbreviated publication-safety rule: agent output is advisory unless a
3437: typed evidence surface and deterministic gate permit the corresponding claim;
3438: \item an explicit pointer to
3439: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, and to the pinned discovery-gate
3440: source interface at commit \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b};
3441: \item the four case-row names from Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}: Newmath
3442: intake isolation, upper-fibers overlap block, fake-extension block, and Rule110
3443: limitation gate;
3444: \item the evidence-level boundary that path-verified rows support reviewability
3445: and path completeness, not command-run validation, fresh full-source rebuilds,
3446: daemon execution, or Rule110 dynamic reruns; and
3447: \item the supplemental-upload pointer to \path{review_bundle/}, which carries
3448: the source snapshot, source-interface record, case-evidence manifest, and longer
3449: technical account.
3450: \end{enumerate}
3451: The current short source satisfies this checklist in its structured-source,
3452: publication-safety, and review-artifact paragraphs.  The checklist is the
3453: submission contract; it replaces any need to treat this supplement's metadata
3454: lemmas as part of the two-page presentation claim.
3455: 
3456: The venue facts used for the following local-readiness gate were checked on
3457: 2026-06-04 and rerun on 2026-06-05 local time against the official CICM 2026 call for papers at
3458: \url{https://cicm-conference.org/2026/cicm.php?event=&menu=cfp}, the EasyChair
3459: submission page named by the call at
3460: \url{https://easychair.org/conferences/?conf=cicm2026}, and the Springer LNCS
3461: author-guideline page linked from that call at
3462: \url{https://www.springer.com/gp/computer-science/lncs/conference-proceedings-guidelines}.
3463: The local checks recorded HTTP status 200 for all three pages in the run witness
3464: \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log} and in the later live-check witness
3465: \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-06_ROUND5.log}, both with exit code 0, and observed the same route
3466: facts as the 2026-06-03 near-submission check: presentation-only papers are
3467: work-in-progress submissions of two pages plus bibliography, receive a
3468: light-weight review, do not appear in the Springer proceedings, have a
3469: 2026-06-15 submission deadline, and use the EasyChair/LNCS submission interface
3470: unless the final form gives a different instruction.  These facts are inputs to
3471: a local submission-pack readiness check only; if upload occurs after this check
3472: or the form shows a conflicting instruction, the finite venue gate must be run
3473: again and, if venue compliance is to be certified inside the submitted package,
3474: the updated venue record and log must be included in the submitted support
3475: surface.  Otherwise these file names record historical local readiness only.
3476: 
3477: \begin{proposition}[Fresh venue-record criterion]
3478: \label{audit:fresh-venue-record-criterion}
3479: Assume that the final submitted support surface contains either the venue-check
3480: record \path{VENUE_CHECK.md} and log \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log}, the later local-date rerun log
3481: \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-06_ROUND5.log}, or an
3482: archive-equivalent replacement naming the same checked URLs, observation time,
3483: route rules, command result, source state, environment, exit code, and log path.
3484: Then the venue row of Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate} is
3485: accepted for the current package only when the upload is made on that checked
3486: rule state or after a later record with the same fields has replaced it.  If
3487: that support surface is not submitted, or if an upload occurs after a venue-page
3488: change without a replacement record, the package retains only a dated local
3489: readiness observation and cannot certify upload-time venue compliance.
3490: 
3491: If the local venue-check files are not part of the submitted support surface,
3492: mentions of \path{VENUE_CHECK.md}, \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log}, and
3493: \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-06_ROUND5.log} in
3494: this supplement are historical/path-verified diagnostic context only, not finite
3495: package coordinates for a venue certificate.
3496: \end{proposition}
3497: 
3498: \begin{proof}
3499: The venue gate uses facts that are external to the manuscript: route class,
3500: length, review status, proceedings boundary, deadline, submission form, style
3501: interface, and software/data-access instruction.  These facts can change outside
3502: the submitted source, so the gate cannot be discharged by a stale table row.
3503: The stated support-surface hypothesis supplies the finite certificate surface:
3504: three official URLs, a date and time, command-result fields, an HTTP-status run
3505: log, observed route facts, and the boundary that a later upload or conflicting
3506: form instruction triggers re-verification.  If the
3507: upload uses that recorded rule state, each venue-dependent row of
3508: Table~\ref{tab:venue-submission-pack-gate} is checked against a dated official
3509: surface.  If the upload occurs after a change, or without a replacement record,
3510: at least one external premise of Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate}
3511: is no longer certified by the package; the venue row is therefore not accepted.
3512: This proves the criterion and connects the fresh record to the local readiness
3513: gate.  Without the support-surface hypothesis, the named local files are not
3514: reviewer-available finite coordinates, so the same text can witness only a dated
3515: historical observation.
3516: \end{proof}
3517: 
3518: \begin{theorem}[Venue-rule freshness non-escalation]
3519: \label{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation}
3520: Let $U$ be the finite dated venue record consisting of the official call
3521: URL, the submission-form URL, the style-guideline URL, the observation date and
3522: time, the HTTP-status run witness \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log} or the later rerun witness
3523: \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-06_ROUND5.log}, the
3524: observed route class, page limit, proceedings boundary, deadline, supplement
3525: access rule, and the local witness rows in Table~\ref{tab:venue-submission-pack-gate}.
3526: The venue component of the current finite-record package is accepted exactly for
3527: uploads whose venue-rule state is represented by $U$ or by a later replacement
3528: record with the same fields.  If an upload is made after a venue-page change, or
3529: if no upload-time replacement record is supplied, then the package retains only a
3530: historical venue observation and cannot certify submission-pack readiness,
3531: EasyChair compliance, acceptance, or proceedings status.
3532: \end{theorem}
3533: 
3534: \begin{proof}
3535: The venue rows used by the package are not internal mathematical definitions;
3536: they are external facts read from the official call, submission, and style
3537: surfaces.  Therefore the finite information available to the package about the
3538: venue is precisely the tuple $U$ displayed in the statement: three source URLs,
3539: one date and time, the HTTP-status run log, the finite route facts observed at
3540: that check, and the corresponding local witness rows.  On the class of uploads
3541: whose venue-rule state is represented by $U$, each premise of the local readiness gate is a component of that tuple, so
3542: Proposition~\ref{audit:fresh-venue-record-criterion} and
3543: Table~\ref{tab:venue-submission-pack-gate} determine the bounded readiness
3544: conclusion.
3545: 
3546: Now suppose the venue page changes before upload or the upload is made without a
3547: replacement record.  At least one external premise--route class, page limit,
3548: deadline, submission interface, proceedings boundary, or supplement rule--is then
3549: not determined by $U$.  Two possible upload states can agree on the historical
3550: record $U$ while differing on that changed premise.  A certificate that is a
3551: function only of $U$ cannot distinguish those two states, so it cannot certify the
3552: stronger upload-time claim.  A later replacement record with the same fields is
3553: exactly the finite enlargement needed to restore determinacy.  The excluded
3554: claims of EasyChair compliance, acceptance, and proceedings status require still
3555: more external records and are not fields of $U$.  Hence stale venue observations
3556: remain historical only, while the accepted venue gate is the fresh finite-record
3557: one stated above.
3558: \end{proof}
3559: 
3560: \begin{corollary}[Historical venue record non-escalation]
3561: \label{cor:historical-venue-record-non-escalation}
3562: The venue check recorded at \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log}, with local
3563: observation time \path{2026-06-04 10:15 Asia/Singapore}, is a historical venue
3564: record.  For the present post-2026-06-04 source state it is superseded, for local
3565: readiness only, by the rerun log
3566: \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-06_ROUND5.log}, whose local
3567: observation time is \path{2026-06-05 12:19 Asia/Singapore} and whose exit code is
3568: \path{0}.  Neither dated record certifies actual upload-time EasyChair
3569: compliance, acceptance, or proceedings status unless the final submitted support
3570: surface also contains either an upload-time record with the same fields or an
3571: explicit statement that the upload used the unchanged venue-rule state represented
3572: by the latest dated record.
3573: \end{corollary}
3574: 
3575: \begin{proof}
3576: Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation} first to the dated
3577: tuple whose HTTP-status witness is \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log}.  The
3578: 2026-06-05 live-check log has the same required fields and is therefore the
3579: later replacement record for local readiness after the source has moved past
3580: 2026-06-04.  Applying the theorem to that latest tuple gives exactly the stated
3581: boundary: the package has a fresh local-readiness observation, but if the final
3582: support surface contains neither an upload-time replacement nor a statement tying
3583: the upload to the unchanged rule state represented by the latest tuple, then the
3584: upload-time venue-rule state is not a finite coordinate of the package.
3585: EasyChair compliance, acceptance, and proceedings status still require external
3586: records not present in either dated tuple.
3587: \end{proof}
3588: 
3589: \begin{corollary}[Upload-time venue-compliance record requirement]
3590: \label{cor:upload-time-venue-compliance-record-requirement}
3591: The logs \path{VENUE_CHECK_RUN_2026-06-04.log} and
3592: \path{review_bundle/VENUE_BIBLIOGRAPHY_LIVE_CHECK_2026-06-06_ROUND5.log} are dated
3593: local-readiness records.  For the current package they certify only the venue
3594: rule state observed at their recorded observation times.  They do not certify
3595: upload-time EasyChair compliance unless the final submitted support surface also
3596: contains either
3597: \begin{enumerate}
3598: \item a later upload-time venue/bibliography record with the same URL,
3599: observation-time, command-result, source-state, environment, exit-code, and
3600: log-path fields; or
3601: \item a finite statement that the completed upload used the unchanged venue-rule
3602: state represented by the latest included dated record.
3603: \end{enumerate}
3604: If neither record is present, all venue-dependent conclusions in
3605: Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate} and
3606: Table~\ref{tab:venue-submission-pack-gate} remain historical local-readiness
3607: claims and cannot be escalated to upload-time compliance, acceptance, or
3608: proceedings-status claims.
3609: \end{corollary}
3610: 
3611: \begin{proof}
3612: Theorem~\ref{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation} identifies the finite venue
3613: record $U$ by its official URLs, observation time, run witness, observed route
3614: facts, and witness rows.  The two named logs instantiate such records at their
3615: own dated observation times.  A final upload-time compliance claim, however,
3616: depends on an external state not contained in those dated logs unless the upload
3617: is explicitly tied to the same unchanged rule state or a later replacement record
3618: is supplied.
3619: 
3620: If one of the two listed records is included, the final-upload premise in
3621: Proposition~\ref{audit:venue-submission-pack-gate} is again a finite coordinate
3622: of the package: either the later record directly supplies the upload-time tuple,
3623: or the unchanged-state statement identifies the upload with the latest included
3624: tuple.  If neither record is included, two upload states can agree with all bytes
3625: of the dated logs while differing on the live venue rule, submission form,
3626: deadline, or supplement-access instruction.  A certificate depending only on the
3627: dated logs cannot distinguish those states.  By the finite-coordinate boundary,
3628: the stronger upload-time, acceptance, and proceedings conclusions therefore do
3629: not follow from the current logs.
3630: \end{proof}
