3836: \begin{proposition}[Formal-source gate instantiation]
3837: \label{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}
3838: For the current presentation package, the Lean-facing assertions in rows
3839: \path{pc3} and \path{pc4} of Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} are accepted
3840: only as bounded contextual interfaces, not as accepted formal-source claims.  The
3841: accepted certificate surface is the traceability row $a_1$, the fake-extension
3842: boundary row $a_5$, Table~\ref{tab:source-interface-record},
3843: \path{source_interface_record.json},
3844: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and
3845: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json}.  These records certify that the
3846: short note may mention the mathlib-free design, marker-audit, and axiom-purity
3847: audit vocabulary only as source-interface context and only with the evidence
3848: boundary stated in rows \path{pc3} and \path{pc4}.  They do not certify a fresh
3849: Lean build, a current full-source marker audit, an absence-of-axioms theorem, or
3850: an accepted axiom-purity pass for the whole newmath tree.  Conversely, a
3851: Lean-facing sentence in this package can become a current accepted formal-source
3852: claim exactly by adding an accepted formal-source certificate row with build-gate
3853: and axiom-purity-gate pass records; if the sentence asserts freshness or currency
3854: of an execution, the row must also include the command, source commit or local
3855: state, environment, exit code, and log path required for command-run evidence.
3856: With the present records, every stronger reading of rows \path{pc3} and
3857: \path{pc4} has a minimal bad subrecord of missing formal-source gates or missing
3858: command-run fields.
3859: \end{proposition}
3860: 
3861: \begin{proof}
3862: By the dispatch rule in the operational interface, an accepted formal-source
3863: claim would have claim kind \emph{formal-source} and therefore would require
3864: pass records for both the build gate and the axiom-purity gate.  The current
3865: acceptance ledger has exactly the six rows $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ displayed in
3866: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}; none has claim kind
3867: \emph{formal-source}.  The machine-readable schema mirrors this fact: its
3868: \path{claim_kind_domain} includes \path{formal-source}, and its
3869: \path{gate_dispatch.formal-source} field names \path{build} and
3870: \path{axiom-purity}, but the current pass-record ledger uses $a_1$ for
3871: traceability, $a_5$ for rejected stronger discovery/theorem-content claims, and
3872: contains no accepted formal-source row with build and axiom-purity pass records.
3873: 
3874: It follows that the only positive certificate available for rows \path{pc3} and
3875: \path{pc4} is contextual.  Row $a_1$ supplies inventory and marker-existence
3876: records for the pinned source-interface snapshot, so the paper may say which
3877: Lean-related source-interface vocabulary is being imported.  Row $a_5$ supplies
3878: the boundary that file churn, compilation, or a fake extension is not theorem
3879: growth or discovery, so the paper may explain why formal-looking material is not
3880: promoted without its gate records.  The certificate schema and primary-claim
3881: inventory make the boundary checkable by naming the absent stronger level: a
3882: formal-source acceptance would need build and axiom-purity pass records, and a
3883: command-run version would additionally need command, source commit, environment,
3884: exit code, and log path.  Since those fields are absent from the current package,
3885: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} rejects the stronger
3886: reading by a finite missing-gate or evidence-level subrecord.  The proposition
3887: therefore certifies exactly the bounded contextual Lean interface and no fresh
3888: Lean build or full-source axiom audit.
3889: 
3890: For the converse boundary, suppose a revised package did add a formal-source
3891: certificate row for one of these sentences.  By the deterministic dispatch rule,
3892: the necessary gate set for the formal-source component is precisely
3893: \(\{\texttt{build},\texttt{axiom-purity}\}\), so pass records for those two gates are
3894: sufficient for the finite schema verifier as far as the formal-source component is
3895: concerned, provided the other coordinates of the record meet the same evidence,
3896: promotion, discovery, and boundary checks as any accepted claim.  If the revised
3897: sentence also says that the build or audit was freshly executed for the current
3898: package, Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} adds exactly the
3899: five command-run fields.  Therefore the present package is not merely silent
3900: about currency: it is a complete negative instantiation of the formal-source
3901: gate, and currency can be claimed only by supplying the missing finite rows.\end{proof}
3902: 
3903: \begin{theorem}[Formal-source promotion obstruction]
3904: \label{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction}
3905: In the current presentation package, no manuscript sentence about the Lean/BEDC
3906: source interface can be promoted from bounded contextual interface evidence to an
3907: accepted formal-source claim unless the certificate is enlarged by an accepted
3908: formal-source row containing build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records.  If a
3909: fresh execution reading is also asserted, the certificate must additionally
3910: contain the command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log
3911: path required for command-run evidence.  With the present six-row package ledger,
3912: every such promotion attempt has a finite bad subrecord and is rejected by the
3913: schema verifier.
3914: \end{theorem}
3915: 
3916: \begin{proof}
3917: The dispatch table sends a formal-source claim to the two gates named in
3918: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}: build and
3919: axiom-purity.  Therefore any accepted formal-source version of rows \path{pc3}
3920: or \path{pc4} would need a certificate whose kind set contains
3921: \emph{formal-source} and whose pass-record field $R$ contains one pass record for
3922: each of these two gates.  By
3923: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the current package has
3924: exactly the accepted certificates $a_1,\ldots,a_6$.  None has claim kind
3925: \emph{formal-source}; none contains build-gate and axiom-purity-gate pass records;
3926: and the only positive Lean-facing surface is the bounded traceability and
3927: source-interface context identified in
3928: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}.
3929: 
3930: If the promoted reading also asserts a fresh Lean build, current full-source
3931: marker audit, full-source axiom audit, or command-run theorem-source check, then
3932: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} requires the command-run
3933: tuple: command, source commit or final package digest manifest, environment, exit code, and log
3934: path.  The boundary fields in Tables~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
3935: and~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory} state that no such command-run tuple is
3936: present for the Lean/BEDC source theorem semantics.  Thus a formal-source
3937: promotion fails check~(iii) of the finite verifier by missing required gate pass
3938: records; a fresh-execution promotion fails check~(vi) by unsupported command-run
3939: escalation.  In either case Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
3940: extracts the failed fields as a finite bad subrecord.  Hence the current package
3941: keeps the Lean/BEDC material at the declared bounded contextual level unless new
3942: formal-source and command-run evidence rows are actually added.\end{proof}
3943: 
3944: \begin{theorem}[Evidence-level overclaim obstruction]
3945: \label{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}
3946: Fix the certificate schema, dispatch function, and evidence-level vocabulary of
3947: this paper.  Let $c$ be a load-bearing claim whose accepted certificate has
3948: level path-verified and whose boundary field $B$ records that no command-run
3949: assertion is made.  Then every stronger reading of $c$ that asserts any of the
3950: following additional conclusions is rejected by the finite schema verifier:
3951: \begin{enumerate}
3952: \item external gate soundness for an arbitrary implementation of the workflow;
3953: \item Lean correctness, full-source rebuild success, or axiom-purity beyond the
3954: recorded formal-source gate fields;
3955: \item end-to-end external semantic validity of artifact data beyond the finite
3956: manifest and evaluator surfaces named in $E$ and $R$;
3957: \item a fresh daemon run, full-source rerun, Rule110 or GroundCompiler rerun, or
3958: journal or workshop acceptance; or
3959: \item bundle-backed reviewability when neither \path{review_bundle/} nor an
3960: archival equivalent carrying the same finite records is in the submitted
3961: artifact boundary.
3962: \end{enumerate}
3963: More precisely, such a stronger reading has an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord of
3964: one of the five types in
3965: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}.  Hence the accepted
3966: claim is rigid at its declared evidence level: path-verified certificate records
3967: cannot be promoted by prose into command-run evidence, semantic validation,
3968: formal-source correctness, or external acceptance.
3969: \end{theorem}
3970: 
3971: \begin{proof}
3972: Let $c^+$ be one of the stronger readings.  It has the same displayed manuscript
3973: sentence as $c$ but a larger claim-kind set, a stronger evidence level, or a
3974: larger submitted-artifact boundary requirement.  The verifier does not inspect
3975: rhetorical emphasis; by Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
3976: it inspects only the finite tuple
3977: $\mathcal C=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ and the finite dispatch table.
3978: 
3979: If $c^+$ asserts external gate soundness for an arbitrary implementation, then
3980: it is not merely the finite certificate-construction claim accepted by
3981: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  It needs pass records showing
3982: that the relevant implementation ran every gate in
3983: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$.  Those records are not supplied by the
3984: path-verified certificate for $c$, so the minimal bad subrecord is a missing
3985: pass-record obstruction of type~2.  If $c^+$ asserts Lean correctness,
3986: full-source rebuild success, or axiom-purity, then it is a formal-source or
3987: command-run claim.  Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation} and
3988: Theorem~\ref{thm:formal-source-promotion-obstruction} identify the required
3989: build, axiom-purity, and command-run fields; when they are absent, the minimal
3990: bad subrecord is type~2 for missing formal-source gates or type~5 for command-run
3991: escalation.
3992: 
3993: If $c^+$ asserts end-to-end semantic validity of artifacts beyond the named
3994: manifest and evaluator surfaces, then it has an artifact component not covered
3995: by the finite status and limitation-ledger records in $R$.  The current
3996: acceptance and certificate ledgers display only the bounded artifact row $a_6$:
3997: its evidence surface is the Rule110 status file, limitation ledgers,
3998: paper-data reference, and review-bundle manifest, and its boundary field says
3999: that no fresh artifact rerun is asserted.  A stronger artifact claim therefore
4000: lacks an assigned artifact pass record, giving type~2,
4001: or asserts a fresh evaluator run without command-run fields, giving type~5.  The
4002: same argument covers a fresh daemon run, full-source rerun, and Rule110 or
4003: GroundCompiler rerun: each is a command-run or artifact-strength escalation and
4004: is rejected by the missing gate or command-run boundary fields.
4005: 
4006: If $c^+$ asserts journal or workshop acceptance, then its acceptance-entry field
4007: would have to contain an editorial or venue acceptance record.  The operational
4008: notion of acceptance entry in this paper is internal and submission-local; the
4009: current ledger contains no external editorial acceptance row.  Thus the stronger
4010: reading either lacks the required route/submission pass record, giving type~2,
4011: or lacks the relevant external evidence surface, giving type~1.  Finally, if a
4012: bundle-backed claim is made while no review bundle or archival equivalent lies
4013: inside the submitted artifact boundary, the evidence item named by the
4014: certificate is absent from $E$ at the declared level, so the minimal bad
4015: subrecord is type~1.  These cases exhaust the displayed stronger readings.
4016: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} then gives the stated
4017: classification and proves rigidity at the declared evidence level.\end{proof}
