4019: \begin{theorem}[Finite-witness manifest-bounded record interface]
4020: \label{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}
4021: This theorem is a manifest-bounded finite-record interface instance of
4022: Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}: it records only the
4023: bounded manifest/evaluator coordinates that may be checked, and it rejects
4024: stronger artifact readings unless the package supplies the additional finite
4025: records required for them, such as semantic-validation pass records or fresh
4026: rerun logs.  The interface domain is the finite set
4027: \[
4028: \mathcal M_{a_6}=\{\text{manifest entry},\text{local paths},
4029: \text{reviewable snapshots},\text{boundary field},
4030: \text{status pass row},\text{limitation-ledger pass row}\}.
4031: \]
4032: No Rule110 or GroundCompiler property outside $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ is asserted or certified; semantic validation outside this finite set requires a new artifact or evaluator-command certificate.
4033: 
4034: For the current presentation package, assuming the named review-bundle records
4035: are supplied as supplemental material or by an accepted archive-equivalent
4036: surface, the finite-witness assertions in rows
4037: \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and \path{pc11} of
4038: Table~\ref{tab:primary-claim-inventory} have the following bounded interface.
4039: The assertions printed in this source are manuscript-source assertions about the
4040: allowed finite-record reading; the path checks against the status files,
4041: limitation ledgers, and machine-readable manifest are supplemental-record
4042: assertions conditional on the named \path{review_bundle/} records being submitted
4043: and matching those paths.
4044: Row \path{pc5} is contextual: it states the evidence discipline that finite
4045: witnesses are bound by manifest rows, evaluator outcomes, and substrate
4046: contracts rather than by Lean alone.  Rows \path{pc6} and \path{pc11} are
4047: accepted only as the bounded artifact lesson $a_6$.  Their named manifest and
4048: evaluator surfaces are \path{manifest:case_evidence[3]}, its
4049: \path{local_paths}, \path{reviewable_snapshots}, and \path{boundary} fields,
4050: the \path{status} and \path{limitation-ledger} pass records for $a_6$ in
4051: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
4052: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_STATUS.md},
4053: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_collision_audit_findings.md},
4054: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_exhaustiveness_audit_findings.md},
4055: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_papers_research_report.md}.
4056: Thus the Rule110/GroundCompiler statement is a finite manifest-bounded artifact
4057: status and limitation-ledger claim, not a claim of Rule110 universality, a fresh
4058: evaluator run, a dynamic behavior theorem, or an unbounded artifact validation.
4059: Its classification is path-complete only for the named finite records in
4060: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  Moreover this bounded interface is invariant under
4061: manifest-record equivalence: any two review states
4062: that agree on the named manifest entry, reviewable snapshots, pass-record rows,
4063: and boundary field receive the same publication certificate from this package.
4064: Therefore no additional Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact property can be derived
4065: from the current certificate unless it is already expressible as one of those
4066: finite manifest-bounded fields or a new artifact/command-run certificate is
4067: added.  In particular, any stronger Rule110 or GroundCompiler reading asserting a
4068: fresh evaluator outcome, semantic validation, dynamic behavior, collision-audit
4069: rerun, exhaustiveness rerun, or universality-level artifact fact requires fresh
4070: evaluator logs with command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path;
4071: manifest rows and historical snapshots alone do not supply such a claim.  The
4072: theorem is exactly a finite-record boundary theorem for the artifact
4073: row $a_6$ in the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}: it preserves the
4074: bounded artifact-limitation lesson and classifies all readings requiring further
4075: artifact evidence as outside the current finite certificate.
4076: \end{theorem}
4077: 
4078: \begin{proof}
4079: The finite-witness vocabulary in \path{pc5} is not itself an artifact pass: it
4080: describes which objects would bind a finite substrate claim.  Under the schema
4081: verifier, such a statement becomes load-bearing only when a finite artifact
4082: certificate supplies the assigned artifact gates.  The dispatch function sends
4083: artifact claims to the status and limitation-ledger gates.  In the current
4084: ledger, the only artifact entry is $a_6$, and
4085: Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} gives its evidence surface: the Rule110
4086: status file, collision and exhaustiveness ledgers, paper-data reference, and the
4087: review-bundle manifest.  The same row records the boundary excluding a fresh
4088: \path{make test-collision-audit} or dynamic artifact rerun.  Therefore the only
4089: coordinates available to the artifact certificate are precisely the finite
4090: coordinates collected in $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.
4091: 
4092: The review bundle supplies a finite external-review surface for precisely that
4093: artifact row.  Proposition~\ref{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}
4094: identifies the Rule110 case manifest entry with four reviewable snapshots: the
4095: status file, the collision-audit findings, the exhaustiveness-audit findings,
4096: and the papers research report.  The current pass-record ledger names the two
4097: gate records used by $a_6$: a \path{status} pass record whose input surface is
4098: the Rule110 status file and paper-data reference, and a
4099: \path{limitation-ledger} pass record whose input surface is the collision and
4100: exhaustiveness audit ledgers.  These are the manifest and evaluator-facing fields
4101: available to the current package, and they are exactly the concrete
4102: representatives of $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  They are finite strings and bundled
4103: snapshots, so Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} can check
4104: them without interpreting generated prose, rerunning an evaluator, or assigning
4105: semantics to Rule110 or GroundCompiler beyond the recorded manifest/evaluator
4106: boundary.
4107: 
4108: No other artifact entry is present.  Therefore any stronger reading of
4109: \path{pc6} or \path{pc11}---for example, that the Rule110 artifact suite was
4110: freshly rerun, that GroundCompiler is semantically validated beyond the manifest
4111: family, or that Lean supplies an unbounded semantic universe for the artifact
4112: boundary---would require a further artifact certificate or command-run fields.
4113: Those fields are absent, and the schema verifier rejects the stronger reading by
4114: a finite missing-record obstruction.  The accepted finite-witness conclusion is
4115: exactly the bounded $a_6$ artifact lesson.
4116: 
4117: It remains to prove the manifest-record invariance assertion.  Let $W$ and $W'$
4118: be two possible review states, for example two submitted bundles or two local
4119: working trees, that agree on the finite coordinates named in the statement: the
4120: manifest entry, the four reviewable snapshots, the two pass-record rows for
4121: $a_6$, and the boundary excluding fresh reruns.  The certificate constructor and
4122: the schema verifier never query any other coordinate of a review state;
4123: equivalently, these are exactly the artifact coordinates of the finite set $F$
4124: from
4125: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  They therefore build the same tuple
4126: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) for $W$ and for $W'$, and
4127: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} returns the same
4128: accepted bounded-artifact decision in both cases.  A dynamic semantic assertion
4129: that could hold in $W$ and fail in $W'$ is not a function of this finite tuple.
4130: It cannot be a consequence of the current publication certificate.  To make such
4131: an assertion load-bearing, the package would have to add a new finite coordinate:
4132: an artifact pass record for the asserted semantic property, or a command-run tuple
4133: recording the relevant evaluator execution.  This proves manifest-record
4134: invariance for the finite-witness manifest interface and shows that the theorem is
4135: only a finite-record boundary result for row $a_6$ and the coordinate set
4136: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$, not an artifact-validation
4137: theorem.\end{proof}
4138: 
4139: \begin{corollary}[Artifact semantic-validity boundary for finite witnesses]
4140: \label{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary}
4141: Let $c$ be a finite-witness artifact claim in the current package whose accepted
4142: certificate uses the artifact row $a_6$, and let
4143: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$ be the manifest-bounded coordinate set of
4144: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface}.  If a proposed
4145: stronger reading $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ asserts semantic validity of the unbounded
4146: Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact, semantic correctness of a dynamic evaluator, or
4147: artifact validity beyond the exact evaluator contract recorded in
4148: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$, then $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ is not certified by the present
4149: manuscript package.  It becomes a certifiable reading only after the package adds
4150: one of the following finite coordinates:
4151: \begin{enumerate}
4152: \item an artifact pass record whose input surface is the exact evaluator
4153: contract and whose boundary field states the semantic property being checked; or
4154: \item a command-run tuple for the evaluator, including source state, environment,
4155: command, exit code, and log path, together with the manifest row tying that run to
4156: the submitted artifact.
4157: \end{enumerate}
4158: Consequently, the assertions in primary rows \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and
4159: \path{pc11} that finite substrates are bounded by manifest rows, evaluator
4160: outcomes, and substrate contracts are valid only as finite-record interface
4161: assertions.  For the Rule110/GroundCompiler case they do not assert unbounded
4162: semantic validity unless the exact evaluator contract and its assigned pass or
4163: command-run record are present on the submitted support surface.
4164: \end{corollary}
4165: 
4166: \begin{proof}
4167: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface} identifies the complete
4168: finite coordinate set available to row $a_6$: the manifest entry, local paths,
4169: reviewable snapshots, boundary field, status pass row, and limitation-ledger pass
4170: row.  Its proof also shows manifest-record invariance: two review states agreeing
4171: on those coordinates produce the same artifact certificate even if they differ in
4172: unrecorded dynamic or semantic facts.  Therefore semantic validity of an
4173: unbounded Rule110/GroundCompiler artifact is not a function of the accepted tuple
4174: unless the semantic evaluator contract itself appears as one of the submitted
4175: finite coordinates.
4176: 
4177: Equivalently, compare the accepted artifact reading $c$ with the stronger reading
4178: $c^{\mathrm{sem}}$ in the preorder of
4179: Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  The stronger reading enlarges at
4180: least one of the typed fields: it adds an artifact-semantic evidence surface, a
4181: new pass record for the evaluator contract, or a command-run evidence level for a
4182: fresh evaluator execution.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, an
4183: accepted stronger reading must satisfy the same finite schema after that
4184: enlargement.  If the package supplies neither an artifact pass record for the
4185: semantic evaluator contract nor a command-run tuple with source state,
4186: environment, command, exit code, and log path, then the enlarged certificate has
4187: a finite bad subrecord: either the required evaluator pass row is absent from
4188: $R$, or the command-run coordinate required at evidence level $\mathsf{command}$
4189: is absent from $B$.  Theorem~\ref{thm:mixed-claim-projection-obstruction} then
4190: prevents the status and limitation-ledger rows from certifying the omitted
4191: semantic component.
4192: 
4193: For the current package, rows \path{pc5}, \path{pc6}, and \path{pc11} are tied to
4194: the bounded artifact lesson $a_6$ and to the finite surfaces listed in
4195: $\mathcal M_{a_6}$.  Those surfaces include manifest and evaluator-facing status
4196: or limitation records, but the command-boundary inventory expressly lacks a fresh
4197: Rule110/GroundCompiler semantic rerun or dynamic evaluator log.  Hence the only
4198: certified conclusion is the finite-record one stated in the corollary.  Any
4199: unbounded semantic-validity claim requires one of the additional finite
4200: coordinates enumerated above, so it lies outside the current certificate.\end{proof}
