4265: \begin{proposition}[Primary-inventory freshness criterion]
4266: \label{prop:primary-inventory-freshness-criterion}
4267: Let $S$ be a later edited version of \path{submission_abstract.tex}, and let
4268: $I$ be a primary-claim inventory with rows of the same form as
4269: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}: each row gives a location in
4270: $S$, a claim text or bounded paraphrase, a row status, a certificate surface, and
4271: an evidence level.  Suppose a manual claim audit of $S$ produces a finite list of
4272: load-bearing assertions and verifies the following three conditions:
4273: \begin{enumerate}
4274: \item every load-bearing assertion in $S$ appears in exactly one row of $I$;
4275: \item each row of $I$ points to a supplement theorem, table row, manifest row, or
4276: bounded-context status that exists in the submitted package; and
4277: \item for every row, the status and evidence level do not exceed what its cited
4278: certificate surface proves under the finite record semantics of
4279: the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}.
4280: \end{enumerate}
4281: Then the row-classification conclusion of
4282: Proposition~\ref{prop:primary-claim-row-classification} holds for the edited
4283: primary artifact $S$ with $I$ in place of the current inventory.  Conversely, if
4284: one of the three audit conditions fails, the edited primary artifact has a finite
4285: review obstruction: an unclassified load-bearing assertion, a stale certificate
4286: pointer, or an evidence-level overclaim.
4287: 
4288: For the present source state after the final short-paper edits on 2026-06-05,
4289: the current inventory is additionally witnessed at command-run freshness level by
4290: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-05.log}.  The
4291: recorded command is
4292: \path{python review_bundle/verify_primary_claim_inventory.py}; the recorded
4293: environment is Python 3.10.11 on Windows 10 in this paper directory; the recorded
4294: exit code is 0; and the log records 135 primary-source lines, 12 inventory rows,
4295: and 0 freshness errors.  This command-run claim is limited to row-span and
4296: certificate-field freshness for the primary inventory; it is not a full semantic
4297: proof of every primary sentence.
4298: \end{proposition}
4299: 
4300: \begin{proof}
4301: Assume the three audit conditions.  By condition~1, the audit list is a finite
4302: partition of the load-bearing assertions of $S$ by inventory row: no assertion is
4303: omitted and no assertion is assigned to two incompatible rows.  By condition~2,
4304: each row has a concrete certificate surface inside the submitted package, so the
4305: row is not supported by prose memory or by an absent local file.  By condition~3,
4306: the certificate surface licenses exactly the row's declared status and evidence
4307: level in the sense of the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}.  Hence each
4308: load-bearing assertion of $S$ is either an accepted certificate row, a bounded
4309: contextual interface row, or an accepted bounded artifact row, according to the
4310: status permitted by its cited certificate.  Since the finite audit list exhausts
4311: all load-bearing assertions, there is no hidden primary assertion left to
4312: classify.
4313: 
4314: For the converse, if condition~1 fails, some load-bearing assertion is missing
4315: from the inventory or is assigned inconsistently, giving an unclassified-row
4316: obstruction.  If condition~2 fails, a row points outside the submitted theorem,
4317: table, manifest, or bounded-context surfaces, giving a stale-pointer obstruction.
4318: If condition~3 fails, the row asserts a stronger status or evidence level than
4319: its certificate permits; the schema-relative finite-record exactness theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, supplies a
4320: finite bad subrecord, and Theorem~\ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}
4321: classifies it as an evidence-level overclaim when the excess is a stronger
4322: verification or command-run reading.  These are finite obstructions read from the
4323: edited source, the inventory row, and the cited certificate surface.
4324: 
4325: For the current command-run instantiation, the cited freshness log is itself a
4326: finite submitted record: it names the command class, environment, primary source,
4327: inventory source, line count, row count, error count, and exit code.  An exit code
4328: 0 with error count 0 is exactly the success condition of the comparison command,
4329: so the command-run freshness statement is licensed at the limited evidence level
4330: declared in the proposition.\end{proof}
